title: Log storage
tags: [[Event log]]
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: What an operation records — the rows it took off, the value an append added, the version it added — and how one write lays it all down.

The log lives in the repository's store, beside the data it describes — one transaction
writes both ([[Store tables]]): a ''label'' holds its name, description, parent and
creation time ([[Labels and contexts]]); an ''operation'' the fields below; each operation the ''snapshot'' rows it took off;
HEAD — the newest operation, the change feed's cursor — is a record of its own. (Watcher
events are not here: they are buffered in memory.) The log is a sequence: operations are
numbered in the order they were written, and none names a parent ([[The operation log]],
[[Why the log stops being a tree]]). The names below — `operation`, `op_snapshot`,
`log_head` — are those of the SQLite tables of before September 2026.

|!Operation field |!Holds |
|`id` |never reused; its order is the log's |
|`label_id` |its innermost label |
|`timestamp` |when it was written (Unix ms): one write's operations share it |
|`origin` |who asked for it ([[Operations and their origin]]) |
|`op_type` |[[Operations and their origin]] |
|`entity_uuid` |the metarecord it changed |
|`field_name` |for a field operation |
|`delta` |what the operation added to its entity's version, modulo 2^53 (a stub has none: its records' deltas are computed from its rows) |
|`reverts_op_id` |for an operation a revert wrote: the one it undid |

The store also keeps each label's operations in order, each label's children, each
metarecord's operations and each origin's, so a label's listing, its nesting, a
metarecord's history and the oldest operations of an origin are each a prefix read; the
newest operations are a backward range, and the operation at a time a binary search over
them (timestamps grow with the ids).

`reverts_op_id` is read by displays and by the undo selection — which is why a revert
records it rather than merely looking like one. Nothing on the write path branches on it,
and it may dangle: pruning and retention may remove the operation it names.

!! Snapshots

Each operation stores the rows it took off its record, in the column form of a field row
([[The column form of a value]]) — its ''before''. The state it left, its ''after'', is
not stored (layout 10, November 2026): nothing needs it, and a write costs a key less.
Of an append alone the value it added is kept, which a revert takes off:

|!Type |!Before |!Added (append only) |
|`create_metarecord` |— |— |
|`delete_metarecord` |every row of the deleted one |— |
|`set_field` |the previous rows of the name |— |
|`append_field` |— |the appended row |
|`delete_field` |the deleted rows |— |
|`file_deleted` |the `mfr_path` row |— |
|`file_moved` |the previous `mfr_path` row |— |
|`file_modified` |`mfr_size`, `mfr_mtime` and the hash rows |— |

What reads an operation's ''after'' therefore asks elsewhere: the revert plan's
`changed_since` warning looks for a later operation on the cell, a redo of the operation (a revert of its revert) excepted (an append and a removal
compare the rows with the value they added or took); the move a revert of `file_moved`
makes goes from where the file is recorded after the move — what the next operation on
its `mfr_path` took off, or the row now ([[Filesystem coordination]]) — to the row the
move took off. The log panel and `mf log show` show the before alone, and
`snapshots_after` is gone from the API ([[Log endpoints]]).

A directory's `file_deleted` gives each descendant its own operation in the same write.

A write that adds a value to, or removes it from, a field of many records logs one entry,
which reads as these operations, one per record ([[Mass operations in the log]]).

!! Versions recomputed

A snapshot row carries no id. The version is never restored from the log either: it is a hash of the content
([[Metarecord version]]), so the state a revert writes has the version it should.
An operation records ''no version'', but the number it added to it: its `delta`, the sum of the
terms of the rows it wrote minus those of the rows it took off, computed from the rows
the write already holds. The one reader — a revert matching a trash entry on the version a
file had before the watcher's flush ([[Filesystem coordination]]) — computes the version
back: the metarecord's version now, with the delta of each of its operations since the
label's first subtracted, newest first. The version being a sum of row terms
([[Metarecord version]]), this is exact, and costs the operations on that one metarecord.
A store of an older layout has no deltas: it is opened with an empty log.

!! One write

Every request that changes metadata goes through one `log::Writer`, in one transaction:
its label is checked or opened ([[Labels and contexts]]); for each change, the affected rows and
the version are read (the before snapshot), the change is applied (the version updated
incrementally), and an operation appended;
finally HEAD is recorded and the transaction commits. Every writer — reconcile included — follows it, which is what
keeps the data and HEAD consistent ([[Writes and atomicity]]).

(An `unknown` operation type exists for an unlogged write — no snapshots, so nothing can
revert it. No endpoint writes one: the `no_log` option it was meant for was never needed.)
